  ## 2\. Perplexity for research when scripting a YouTube video or writing an in-depth LinkedIn article
  
  “I use Perplexity.ai for research—when I’m scripting a YouTube video or writing an in-depth LinkedIn article, I like to add stats/research to my content. Perplexity is great for web research and pulling together stats from verifiable sources (that you can cite). I think the question is if you’re not using AI as a content creator—why?” —Cindy Dodd, 63.4K+ following across platforms
  
  ## 3\. Make.com for automatic podcast transcript reviews
  
  “Recently, I built a Make.com scenario that has Claude automatically review any new podcast recording transcripts. It highlights sections that might be good to trim (parts that are boring, off-topic, repetitive, or technical glitches), which saves me time in post-production. I know I should take a closer look at those sections to improve the listening experience. The same scenario also suggests some natural commercial breaks and generates a ‘teaser script’ to invite listeners to stay tuned. And finally, it highlights a few sections it thinks are really interesting or attention-getting to include in the intro/sizzle-reel for the video version.” —Nick Loper, 61.7K+ following across platforms
  
  ## 4\. Pictory to automatically generate B-roll footage
  
  “I haven’t had much luck in building a video scripting AI that really sounds like me, but I have had success in using Pictory to automatically pull in relevant stock b-roll footage for the videos I create. It reads the script and searches a huge database of footage to create a visually compelling video, which used to take me a ton of time to manually search and embed those clips.” —Loper
  
